**Example: Library Cataloging Service**

This is a service that catalogues books and allows users to search for books by title, author, or ISBN.

**REST API:**

To use this REST API, a client (like a web browser or a mobile app) would send HTTP requests to the server, using different methods for different actions:

* Get books: http://library.example.com/books
* Get book by title: http://library.example.com/books/{title}
* Get book by author: http://library.example.com/books/{author}
* Search for books by ISBN: http://library.example.com/books/ISBN={isbn}

Here's how the API might respond to each request:

* GET /books: [{id: 1, title: "1984", author: "George Orwell"}, {id: 2, title: "To Kill a Mockingbird", author: "Harper Lee"}, ...]
* GET /books/{id}: {"id": 1, "title": "1984", "author": "George Orwell", ...}

As you can see, in a REST API like this, clients need to specify the exact title or author to obtain a book's information; and the server chooses the format and structure of the data returned.

**GraphQL API:**

For the GraphQL API, the query language is used for both server-side and client-side. Both the server and the client or client-side library can use the same syntax for queries and the API returns a standardized result.

Here are some examples of queries that might be asked by clients:

* books that have "1984" in the title:
```graphql
query {
  books(title: "1984") {
    title
    author
  }
}
```

Or the books that have a specific ISBN:

```graphql
query {
  books(isbn: "978-0400001009") {
    title
    author
  }
}
```

As you can see, in a GraphQL API, clients can specify a wide range of queries in a single request; and the server will return exactly the types of data they asked for.

Here are the responses in both APIs:

* REST API: {"id": 1, "title": "1984", "author": "George Orwell"}, {"id": 2, ...}
